from: benchmark
to: planner
session: overnight campaign 2026-08-10 — guard tooling, harness fixes, scenario matrix

Dean approved autonomous cluster use overnight (including the un-pause) and asked for the GPUs to be
freed when done. Full state: `plans/session/status/benchmark.md` §20 (authoritative copy).

## 1. Three harness blockers fixed — none of them known before tonight

Each was found by running, and each one blocked *all* load generation. Committed separately with the
reasoning, since the distinctions are not guessable and each cost an attempt:

1. **`BENCHMARK_WORKLOAD` is an upstream-catalog name, fetched over the network.** It is **not** the
   way to select one of our profiles. Ours live in `hack/benchmark/workloads/`, are synced into the
   harness, and are chosen by the scenario's `harness.experimentProfile` — which was **hardcoded**, so
   a per-cell load shape was not expressible at all. Now driven by a new `BENCHMARK_PROFILE`.
2. **`PyYAML` missing from the system `python3`** while the benchmark venv has 6.0.3. The Makefile had
   already solved this for plotting via `PLOT_PYTHON`; added `YAML_PYTHON` on the same principle
   (choose the interpreter that can actually `import yaml`). **Fixed for the class** — three helpers
   import yaml and all three were invoked as bare `python3`, so the same abort was waiting in the
   analysis step and in variant configuration.
3. **A token cannot be used for `experimentProfile`** — my own first fix, wrong. `sync_workloads.py`
   validates that field by reading the **source** scenario, *before* substitution, so a `__TOKEN__`
   there fails validation as a literal profile name. Every other token in that file is only read
   post-substitution, which is why the pattern works for them. Now an anchored `sed` rewrite of the
   line in the harness copy, with the constraint documented at the line so nobody re-tokenises it.

**Planner note:** (1) and (3) are the kind of thing worth capturing in the Type 3's tooling track —
they are latent traps for anyone adding a workload profile or a scenario value, not one-off slips.

## 2. Guard tooling landed (Dean's design, settled in conversation)

`env_guard.py` / `env_wizard.py` / `apply_images.py`, wired into the **10 destructive** targets only;
read-only targets deliberately ungated. Design recorded in
`plan__benchmark-env-guard-design.md`; per Dean's corrections it is **guards, not tiers**, with
`UNSAFE=confirm|once|silent` as a first-class escape hatch and named `X.env` files carrying
`KUBE_CONTEXT` inside for verification.

`apply_images.py` closed a real gap: the image pin previously reached the cluster **only** via
standup, so an A/B across controller images had no clean path. **Verified working for real** — it
rolled the controller `ta-0.9-anchor-20260807 → ta-0.9-anchor-pr2-20260809` and re-observed the
running pod.

Also landed: `hack/benchmark/campaign/` drivers (were in a gitignored dir and would have been lost).

## 3. Autoscaling confirmed working on the PR-2 image

The validation cell generated load and the controller **scaled 1 → 2 replicas** (desired raised
03:52:20, second replica ready 03:53:12). This is the first end-to-end confirmation that the
new image autoscales under load in this namespace.

## 4. ⚠️ What is NOT established — do not read the cell names as findings

The `m-ta-*` cells are **configured** throughput-only and **named** for what Dean asked for. Whether
the engine actually stops prepending the saturation result is **what the runs test**, not something
observed. Every idle-window observation so far (`2 saturation + 2 throughput` with no load) says
nothing either way. `run_cell.sh` records per-analyzer `analyzer-result` counts from each cell's saved
controller log — that is the evidence to read.

Dean's instruction was explicit: *"PR-2 should fix it — allow real disable. Do not verify, just test
as is with the available image."* So this is configured-as-intended and reported-as-observed.

## 5. Method note worth keeping

Switching from "launch all 7" to "validate 1 cell first" is what turned a 7×-repeated failure into
three cheap single-cell diagnoses. The sequencer now also aborts on the first resultless cell. Worth
making the default for future campaigns.
